@startuml
!include style/ail.puml
title G7 · Callback timing: call scope versus owner scope
hide footbox
$caller(client, "Client\nCalls registration and teardown")
$callee(api, "API\nInvokes the handler")
$callee(handler, "Handler\nRuns one callback invocation")
== Constructed call-scoped operation ==
note over client, api : Registration enters: borrow handler, retain none.
api -[dotted]> handler : Start during the registration call
activate handler
note over handler : Invocation completes.
deactivate handler
note over client, api : Registration completes: no invocation running or able to start.
note over api, handler
  scope(call); count(1, 1); quiescence(return).
  Exactly one invocation, conditional on eventual scope end.
end note
== Synthetic owner-scoped subscription: successful registration ==
note over client, api : subscribe enters: borrow handler and retain for owner($result).
api -> client : subscribe returns: give one owned Subscription
note over api, handler : Only now may invocations start: timing(after_return).
api -[dotted]> handler : One permitted start (zero or more over owner scope)
activate handler
note over client, handler
  Client enters unsubscribe outside this callback.
  An invocation may still be in flight on another thread.
end note
client -> api : unsubscribe entry: consume Subscription
note over handler : In-flight invocation completes during teardown.
deactivate handler
note over client, api : unsubscribe returns: no invocation running or able to start.
note over api, handler
  scope(owner($result)); count(0, many); quiescence(@unsubscribe).
  One permitted trace, not a promised delivery or termination guarantee.
end note
legend bottom
  Time runs downward within separate cases; spacing is not duration.
  Actor = caller; rectangles = callees (API becomes caller when invoking Handler).
  Bars = active handler invocations; dotted arrow = callback entry; solid arrow = ownership transfer.
  Notes mark completion; no arrow denotes an ordinary return. Non-normative: C3, C6, F1.
  outside_callback prevents blocking teardown from the callback it waits for.
  Native scheduling and capture-release machinery are omitted.
endlegend
@enduml
